WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.160
A wise someone once said&nbsp;
“What has been will be again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:08.640
what has been done will be done again;&nbsp;
there is nothing new under the sun.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:13.920
And by someone, I mean the Book of Ecclesiastes&nbsp;
from the Tanakh, or the Christian Old Testament.

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:20.000
And this bit of knowledge has maintained&nbsp;
its relevance more than 2000 years later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:24.560
especially when it comes to the fiction we&nbsp;
enjoy. And I’m not saying that because I’m&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:29.840
rereading the latest New York Time’s Best&nbsp;
Seller retelling of Pride and Prejudice.

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:34.720
But from James Joyce’s Ulysses&nbsp;
to Bridget Jones’s Diary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:39.200
you’ve probably read a book that was just&nbsp;
a modern retelling of a well-established&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:44.480
story. Which is to say nothing of other forms of&nbsp;
media and their own obsessions with retellings.

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:48.720
And despite what your Writing&nbsp;
101 instincts might tell you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:52.960
this is neither bad nor lazy&nbsp;
writing—or even a new concept.&nbsp;

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:58.080
Because let’s be honest: sometimes a story&nbsp;
is just so danged good, it bears repeating.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:02.800
Sometimes more than once. Sometimes multiple&nbsp;
times. I’m looking at you, Jane Austen.

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:08.080
So why do we retell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:08.764
stories? In the words of Oscar Wilde,&nbsp; “The&nbsp;
originality which we ask from the artist is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.764 --> 00:01:08.904
originality of treatment, not of subject. It&nbsp;
is only the unimaginative who ever invent. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.904 --> 00:01:09.040
true artist is known by the use he makes of&nbsp;
what he annexes, and he annexes everything.”

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:15.120
To put it simply, in Wilde’s words, “There is&nbsp;
art found not in a work’s perceived originality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:18.640
but in the way the artist has&nbsp;
used (or deliberately not used)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:23.520
and arranged pre-existing thoughts and motifs&nbsp;
to make something that is entirely their own.”

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:28.320
So with that, let’s take a look&nbsp;
at three authors who’ve engaged in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:35.920
literary upcycling and compare. Starting with the&nbsp;
Bard himself, William Shakespeare. That’s right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:40.720
our Billy Boy would probably be rocking&nbsp;
an Archive of Our Own account today.

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:44.880
Written centuries before Wattpad was a&nbsp;
twinkle in the internet’s collective eye,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:48.400
many of Shakespeare’s works are&nbsp;
retellings of historical events&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:52.480
or common narratives that were already&nbsp;
well-established in Elizabethan England.

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:56.320
According to Professor Gordon&nbsp;
McMullan, director of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.320 --> 00:02:01.440
London Shakespeare Centre at King’s College:
“With almost no exception, [Shakespeare] got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:06.960
his stories from elsewhere and reworked them&nbsp;
in his own way. The point was, everyone did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:12.000
this. The concept of originality, like genius,&nbsp;
was invented at the end of the 18th century.”&nbsp;

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:21.280
Even works as ubiquitous and widely-taught as&nbsp;
Romeo and Juliet and Hamlet are a retelling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:28.000
of some sort. The former being based on a&nbsp;
1562 English translation of an Italian poem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:32.880
The Tragical History of Romeus and Juliet&nbsp;
by Arthur Brooke. The latter being derived&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:38.720
primarily from the medieval Scandinavian legend of&nbsp;
Amleth—and, perhaps more alluringly, from what is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:46.080
referred to Ur-Hamlet—a play of unknown origin&nbsp;
published more than a decade earlier in 1587,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:51.280
which some scholars believe may have been written&nbsp;
by Thomas Kyd, Shakespeare’s contemporary.

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:56.960
Then there are his historical plays, which take a&nbsp;
…. very loose but poetic take on real-life figures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:04.400
like Julius Caesar and King Richard III. BTS&nbsp;
fan fiction has nothing on Shakespeare’s take on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:10.480
Henry V, for example—reshaping the Lancaster&nbsp;
monarch of England as not just a war hero,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:15.600
but as a complex and sometimes unscrupulous&nbsp;
ruler whose military ambitions come at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:21.440
cost of tremendous violence and bloodshed. Whole&nbsp;
academic careers have been devoted to the study of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:26.160
Shakespeare’s Henriad plays, dissecting&nbsp;
how the text paralleled and prophesied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:30.880
events in the playwright’s contemporary&nbsp;
England and the rise of the Tudor dynasty.

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:36.080
And yet, as much as we think of them as high&nbsp;
literature today, Shakespeare’s works at the time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:42.960
were seen as popcorn for the unwashed masses: that&nbsp;
is, easily available, written in language that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:48.320
easily understandable to poor, uneducated&nbsp;
folk, and *gasp* cheap rehashes.

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:52.960
Professors Jessica McCall and Kavita&nbsp;
Mudan Finn perhaps capture best the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:59.440
uncanny parallels between how Shakespeare’s&nbsp;
retellings and modern “fan fiction”, saying

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:05.280
“Amongst fans and the academics who study them,&nbsp;
it is generally accepted (perhaps even a truth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:10.960
universally acknowledged) that a good portion of&nbsp;
what we consider canonical literature – including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:17.440
Shakespeare – also fits the broadest definition&nbsp;
of fanfiction, in that it is clearly written in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:23.600
response to or adapting a specific source text.&nbsp;
Although it is difficult to reconcile any category&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:30.160
that comfortably includes both Shakespeare’s plays&nbsp;
and E.L. James’ Fifty Shades of Grey – 89 per cent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:35.600
of which had already appeared on the Internet as&nbsp;
a piece of Twilight fanfiction entitled ‘Master&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:40.960
of the Universe’ – some have argued that this&nbsp;
juxtaposition is the very point of fandom.”&nbsp;

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:45.440
But Shakespeare was hardly the first&nbsp;
or last person to throw his hat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:48.560
into the retelling ring and come out the victor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:54.000
nor was he the only person making modernized&nbsp;
hay out of antiquity—and we can’t talk about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:59.840
modernized retellings without talking about James&nbsp;
Joyce and the sprawling work that is his Ulysses.

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:07.120
Ulysses is based on that flagship of high school&nbsp;
literature, The Odyssey, Homer’s ancient Greek&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:13.200
epic poem concerning its titular hero, Odysseus,&nbsp;
and the many perils he faces trying to return&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:19.040
home to his kingdom, Ithaca. and FYI, Ulysses&nbsp;
is just the Roman version of the name Odysseus.

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:28.240
A fixture of study, Joyce was hardly the first&nbsp;
person to grapple with retelling The Odyssey.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:32.720
However, what Joyce ended up creating&nbsp;
is considered one of the most important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:35.200
modernist texts of the 20th century.

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:41.120
In place of Odysseus is Leopold Bloom, and&nbsp;
instead of spanning a decade in ancient Greece,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:47.360
his adventures occur over one June day in modern&nbsp;
Dublin. Where the Odyssey is comprised of 24&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:53.920
chapters, Ulysses roughly corresponds to them in&nbsp;
eighteen stream-of-conscious segments, brimming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.920 --> 00:06:00.720
with characters and plot situations that are all&nbsp;
directly lifted from the source—your Penelopes and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:07.440
Stephen Dedaluses and whatnot. But Joyce uses&nbsp;
these similarities to convert Odysseus’s trials&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:13.680
into the psychological feeling of being lost—&nbsp;
lost to modern conceptions of day-to-day living,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:19.520
lost to feelings of national identity, lost in&nbsp;
Ireland’s relationship to the United Kingdom.&nbsp;

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:25.520
In his 1980 introduction to the work, Canadian&nbsp;
literary scholar William Hugh Kenner remarked:

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:30.480
“[Ulysses and The Odyssey] are identical in&nbsp;
not existing, save as manifestations of human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:35.600
creative power, products of an artistic&nbsp;
process that is like a natural process,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:43.520
and that intuits in many times comparable&nbsp;
situations, whether in the mind of Homer of Chios&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:49.600
or in the mind of James Augustine Joyce. Both,&nbsp;
far from the scenes of their stories, created&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:54.800
their heroes and re-created an epic geography…&nbsp;
Both were guided by the circumstances they knew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.440 --> 00:07:02.240
(warfare, seafairing, marvels;&nbsp;
commerce, perambulation, novelties).”

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:06.800
All this is to say that James Joyce is what the&nbsp;
kids might refer to as the true godfather of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:12.560
the modern AU—that is, the writer who is able&nbsp;
to take a story older than written tradition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.560 --> 00:07:16.400
and deftly draw significance from&nbsp;
both the similarities and differences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:23.440
by placing it in a new setting. So this is why&nbsp;
I toil and labor over my Game of Thrones coffee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:28.080
shop AU. Sansa Stark finding agency through her&nbsp;
day shift at a Starbucks has meaning, damn it.

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:34.400
Speaking of female desire, misunderstood by&nbsp;
audiences, no video essay about modern retellings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:40.000
would be complete without talking about the queen&nbsp;
herself, Jane Austen. And the profound legacy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:44.640
she has left to literature and its fascination&nbsp;
with retellings. I bring up Austen not because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:50.160
her novels are specifically retellings—though she&nbsp;
herself often used well-worn romantic and comedic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:56.000
contrivances that had long existed in literature&nbsp;
and contemporized them for her readers—but because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.000 --> 00:08:02.880
of the profound impact her body of work had on&nbsp;
the modern commercial novel and media in general.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:08.880
From Bridget Jones’s Diary to Clueless to Sense&nbsp;
and Sensibility and Sea Monsters, if we have one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:15.280
socially acceptable cache of stories for retelling&nbsp;
outside of Shakespeare, it’s our girl Jane.

00:08:15.280 --> 00:08:20.560
I don’t think there’s a way to capture this&nbsp;
vibe succinctly, so here are just (some) of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:24.480
the 20th and 21st novels that are direct&nbsp;
retellings of her work. This doesn’t even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:29.840
begin to touch on film, comic book, and&nbsp;
television re-iterations of her works, in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.840 --> 00:08:36.240
which case we’d have an eight-hour long episode.&nbsp;
And this is publicly funded programming, okay?

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:44.560
Arguably the most financially successful from&nbsp;
this list is Helen Fielding’s Bridget Jones’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:49.920
Diary and its sequel, The Edge of Reason,&nbsp;
based on Austen novels Pride and Prejudice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:55.840
and Persuasion. Though the Bridget Jones character&nbsp;
existed before the publication of both books as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.840 --> 00:09:00.640
recurring fictional columnist for The Independent,&nbsp;
when writing her book, Fielding saw the parallels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:06.320
between Jones’ obsession with modern femininity,&nbsp;
fashion, and dating and the way Austen’s Elizabeth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.320 --> 00:09:11.600
Bennet finds herself constantly struggling&nbsp;
with the social mores of regency England.

00:09:11.600 --> 00:09:16.320
Said Fielding, rather cheekily, when asked if&nbsp;
she had lifted the plot of her books from Austen:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.120 --> 00:09:22.320
“I thought both plot and leading man had been&nbsp;
well market-researched over a number of centuries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:28.160
and that Jane Austen wouldn't mind. (Anyway, she's&nbsp;
dead.) Austen wrote about the minutiae of women's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.160 --> 00:09:34.400
lives in a way that is funny and dazzlingly&nbsp;
accurate, giving you insights into what was going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.400 --> 00:09:39.680
on in the social world without your even realizing&nbsp;
you're getting an incredible history lesson.”

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:45.360
But we reach a conundrum here, despite Bridget&nbsp;
Jones’s Diary’s success, there is a bigger issue.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:49.120
For all of the Austen retellings&nbsp;
that exist in the market,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.120 --> 00:09:54.320
almost all of them are relegated to the dreaded&nbsp;
genre of “chick lit” or “women’s literature”.

00:09:57.120 --> 00:10:02.880
Why is it that, if we can peg down Shakespeare and&nbsp;
James Joyce as artistically acceptable examples of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.880 --> 00:10:08.400
literary recyclists, that we look at books like&nbsp;
Bridget Jones’s Diary or Jenny Lee’s best-selling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:14.960
retelling of Anna Karenina, Anna K., as disposable&nbsp;
or *gasp* barely a step above fan fiction.

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:20.640
We’ve already done an episode in&nbsp;
part on this—click the link above.

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:26.080
But to be succinct, retellings, especially ones&nbsp;
sold as retellings, are a huge part of genres that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.080 --> 00:10:35.840
tend to skew towards audiences with women, LGBTQ+,&nbsp;
and BIPOC audiences. And that’s a whole basket of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.840 --> 00:10:42.480
conceptually difficult apples to unpack. We’d be&nbsp;
here picking through those dynamics for another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.480 --> 00:10:48.960
twelve hours.&nbsp; ( Another public funding/‘viewers&nbsp;
like you’ joke). But I think these actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.960 --> 00:10:53.520
speak to a larger truth about WHY retellings&nbsp;
exist and why we keep coming back to them.

00:10:54.240 --> 00:11:00.960
Says scholar Richa Nagar:
Stories shape our sense of self: who we believe we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:06.800
are, our sense of our own inherited privileges and&nbsp;
misfortunes, our affinities, identifications, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.800 --> 00:11:13.360
allegiances. Stories also shape what we wish or&nbsp;
hope to do in order to give our existence meaning:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.360 --> 00:11:20.000
whom we want to stand or fight with, whom or&nbsp;
what we want to help or rescue in order to uphold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:26.080
what we think is ethical or just…. Telling&nbsp;
stories involves making decisions or moves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.080 --> 00:11:31.520
and each retelling of a familiar story may either&nbsp;
give birth to new meanings, nuances, and affects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.080 --> 00:11:36.960
or erase their possibility. Thus, each&nbsp;
storyteller can be seen as a translator&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.960 --> 00:11:42.640
of stories with a responsibility to retell&nbsp;
stories in ways that can do justice to them.&nbsp;

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:48.400
That is, that the saying “there is nothing new&nbsp;
under the sun” is not an inherently negative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.400 --> 00:11:55.440
statement—rather, that stories persevere because&nbsp;
they have some universality to them that has their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.440 --> 00:12:02.640
time and place as society moves away and towards&nbsp;
them again. The minutiae may change, the trappings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.640 --> 00:12:08.000
different, but when we retell a story, we are&nbsp;
asked to consider its power. What does a story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:15.120
say about where we came from? What does it mean&nbsp;
about where we are now, and where we are going?

00:12:15.120 --> 00:12:17.520
And while necessity might&nbsp;
be the mother of invention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.080 --> 00:12:21.040
it is in repetition we find those truths.

00:12:21.040 --> 00:12:24.880
All this to say, please pre-order my&nbsp;
upcoming reimagining of Frog and Toad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.880 --> 00:12:27.120
titled Gerbil and Hamster.

